From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tinycc-os-kernel <tinycc-os-kernel@localhost>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 13:59:57 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] tccasm: GNU as binary constants (0b1010) independent of the
 libc

The assembler left "0b..." numbers to strtoull(), which with glibc 2.38+
accepts the C23 binary prefix (so "pshufd $0b11111010" in the kernel's
sha256-ssse3-asm.S assembled by luck) and with musl does not: there the
constant parsed as 0 followed by 'b', a backward reference to local
label 0, and tcc built against musl (the self-hosting image's compiler)
failed with "local label '0' not found backward".  Parse 0b/0B followed
by a binary digit as a base-2 constant explicitly; "0b" alone remains
the local label reference.  Byte-compared with GNU as on binary
constants and local label references; the musl tcc now produces the
same object as the glibc one for the SHA-256 file.
---
 tccasm.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/tccasm.c b/tccasm.c
index c371855..0b38ea4 100644
--- a/tccasm.c
+++ b/tccasm.c
@@ -400,7 +400,14 @@ static void asm_expr_unary(TCCState *s1, ExprValue *pe)
     switch(tok) {
     case TOK_PPNUM:
         p = tokc.str.data;
-        n = strtoull(p, (char **)&p, 0);
+        /* GNU as binary constants ("pshufd $0b11111010" in the kernel's
+           SHA-256 code); not left to strtoull, which accepts them with
+           glibc 2.38+ (C23) and not with musl.  "0b" alone is the
+           backward reference to local label 0, handled below. */
+        if (p[0] == '0' && (p[1] == 'b' || p[1] == 'B') && (p[2] == '0' || p[2] == '1'))
+            n = strtoull(p + 2, (char **)&p, 2);
+        else
+            n = strtoull(p, (char **)&p, 0);
         /* C integer suffixes, as GNU as accepts them ("$0x0700a169U" in
            the kernel's generated feature masks) */
         while (*p == 'U' || *p == 'u' || *p == 'L' || *p == 'l')
